def cite(k):
 a,t,u,loc,e=refs[k];return f'<a href="#ref-{k}">{html.escape(a)}</a>'
def code(t):return '<code>'+html.escape(t)+'</code>'
modules=[]
def add(id,title,status,verdict,sources,rows,keep,snippet): modules.append(dict(id=id,title=title,status=status,verdict=verdict,sources=sources,rows=rows,keep=keep,snippet=snippet))
add('P01','Well-posedness, positivity and absorption','Completed body','Compress the general ODE machinery; retain the DM inequalities.',['GH','Buse','Guil'],[
('lem:P01-max','Short direct fact','State the finite-maximum/Dini derivative identity. A short active-index argument is enough; the review supplies no checked exact citation for this lemma.'),
('prop:P01-local','Standard theorem + check','Invoke local existence/uniqueness and continuation for a smooth vector field. Keep the open domain and Fᵢ|xᵢ=0=cxᵢ₋₁≥0. Do not reprove ODE theory.'),
('prop:P01-positive','Short calculation','Keep the integrating-factor representation and the propagation along the predecessor cycle. Cite standard positivity/comparison machinery only as background.'),
('lem:P01-bound; thm:P01-global','Standard theorem + calculation','Keep H(X), its root R and the Dini inequality. Invoke scalar comparison and continuation; remove the extended scalar comparison proof. Retain a displayed estimate for every rate or common entry time actually claimed.'),
('thm:P01-absorption','Retain model-specific proof','No reviewed paper establishes this exact iff statement. Keep the strict-loss argument and exceptional zero-coefficient counterexamples if you keep the full weak-sign theorem.'),
('cor:P01-canonical; prop:P01-kzero','Internal corollary / short estimate','The canonical result follows from the preceding bound. For k=0 use D⁺X≤(a+c)X under the stated nonnegative loss coefficients; exponential control suffices for continuation.')],
'The exact radius, propagation of strict positivity, finite entry into K_R, and distinctions between attraction and absorption remain yours. Bușe et al. and Guilberteau are analogous applications, not replacement theorems. The audit’s textbook reference is usable for general ODE foundations, but supplies no verified theorem locator for the Dini comparison; retaining two or three lines is preferable to a loose attribution.',
'On (−1,∞)ⁿ the field is smooth, and its boundary values satisfy Fᵢ(x)|xᵢ=0=cxᵢ₋₁≥0. Thus standard ODE invariance and continuation results apply. For X=maxᵢxᵢ, direct estimation gives D⁺X≤H(X)−gX/(1+X)≤H(X), where H(X)=aX−kX²+cX/(1+X). Scalar comparison yields X(t)≤max{X(0),R}, where H(R)=0 and R>0, and hence global continuation. The additional finite-entry conclusion follows from the retained strict g-loss estimate.')
add('P02','Synchrony and equilibrium roots','Completed body','Use a one-line invariance argument and a compact algebraic classification.',['SGP','Stewart'],[
('prop:P02-invariance','Standard framework / direct substitution','F(s1)=f(s)1 proves invariance by uniqueness; alternatively cite fixed-point-space invariance for cyclic equivariance. The entire coupled-cell theory need not be reproduced.'),
('prop:P02-equation; thm:P02-complete; thm:P02-positive','Short algebra, not a borrowed theorem','Display the quadratic, roots, discriminant and positive-root table. Replace case-by-case prose by Vieta signs and domain filtering.'),
('prop:P02-loci; cor:P02-canonical','Internal algebraic corollary','Obtain g_c, g_f and the parameter intervals directly from the quadratic. Refer back to one root table instead of reproving each version.')],
'Keep the denominator restriction s>−1, the persistent zero root, the distinction between positive and merely real roots, and the two-positive-root interval. A related paper’s equilibrium theorem cannot substitute for this different quadratic.',
'By cyclic equivariance the fixed-point line Fix(P)={s1} is invariant (Stewart, §3.3); direct substitution gives f(s)=s(μ+bs−As²)/(1+s), with A=k+h, b=a−A and μ=a+c−g. Nonzero equilibria therefore satisfy As²−bs−μ=0. The stated classification follows from the quadratic formula, Vieta’s relations and the restriction s>−1.')
add('P03','Origin spectrum and homogeneous Jacobian','Completed body','Keep differentiation; cite circulant theory for everything after it.',['Gray'],[
('lem:P03-jacobian; prop:P03-homogeneous','Short model-specific differentiation','Retain the four Jacobian coefficients, modular Kronecker notation or an explicit instruction to add coincident offsets, and the positive sign of the g input derivative.'),
('prop:P03-origin-spectrum; prop:P03-stability; prop:P03-boundary','Direct citation + substitution','Apply Gray’s Theorem 3.1 to J₀=(a−g)I+cP; read stability and the simple-zero gap from the eigenvalues. Keep nonlinear boundary conclusions separate.')],
'You need not prove roots-of-unity diagonalization here and again in P04. The origin’s unique largest real part, threshold μ=0 and gap c[1−cos(2π/n)] follow in a few lines. The exact nonlinear field and its derivative signs are not supplied by Gray.',
'Direct differentiation gives J₀=(a−g)I+cP. By Gray’s circulant diagonalization theorem, λₘ=a−g+ce^(−iθₘ). Since c>0, mode zero uniquely maximizes the real part. At g=a+c the uniform zero eigenvalue is simple and all other real parts are at most −c[1−cos(2π/n)].')
add('P04','General Fourier spectrum, normality and stability','Completed body','The largest clean citation replacement: reduce to one framework lemma.',['Gray','GH'],[
('thm:P04-spectrum; prop:P04-multiplicity; prop:P04-normality','Direct theorem application','Cite Gray, Theorem 3.1. Give the symbol; state that multiplicity equals the number of labels with that value and all eigenvalues are semisimple.'),
('cor:P04-stability','Immediate corollary','Keep the single equality ‖exp(tJ)‖₂=exp(t max Re λₘ). No separate proof of real-versus-complex induced norms is needed.'),
('prop:P04-hyperbolicity','Definition, no proof required','State the distinction between λₘ≠0 (invertibility) and Re λₘ≠0 (hyperbolicity).'),
('eq:P04-synchronous-derivative; eq:P04-equilibrium-zero-mode','Short model calculation','Retain λ₀=f′(x*) and its branch-specific expression. This is the useful connection to P02/P09/P10.'),
('Nonlinear implications; prop:P04-step-cycles','Standard theorem / elementary fact','Cite the linearized stability and instability principles. State the step-q gcd cycle count briefly; no long number-theory proof is needed.')],
'Keep the shift convention, imaginary-part sign, modular collisions and both self-conjugate modes. Normality applies to homogeneous Jacobians; it cannot be silently transferred to a heterogeneous Jacobian. Zero spectral abscissa does not settle nonlinear stability.',
'By Gray (2006, Theorem 3.1), the circulant J=dI+CP+GP^(−q)−HP^q has eigenvalues λₘ=d+Ce^(−iθₘ)+Ge^(iqθₘ)−He^(−iqθₘ) in the orthonormal Fourier basis. Normality and semisimplicity follow, and unitary functional calculus gives ‖exp(tJ)‖₂=exp(t maxₘ Re λₘ). The usual linearization principles give the local nonlinear conclusions when the spectral abscissa is strictly negative or positive.')
add('P05','q=2 mode selection and physical realizability','Completed body','Credit the method, shorten the quadratic argument, preserve the constrained constructions.',['VKS','Stewart'],[
('thm:P05-selection','Method citation + short independent proof','Cite van Kekem–Sterk, Proposition 5, as a close selection precedent. Complete the square for the DM coefficients and state midpoint cells; their fixed Lorenz–96 vertex does not prove your moving-vertex atlas.'),
('thm:P05-reachable','Retain concise proof','The positivity-restricted representative list and parity endpoints require your trigonometric sign analysis.'),
('thm:P05-realization; eq:P05-equilibrium-chart; eq:P05-level-set','Retain model-specific proof','Keep the positive parameter lift, regular branch chart and nonzero level-set derivative. Invoke IFT/regular-level-set theory only for the general conclusion.'),
('eq:P05-stable-switch','Retain construction','Stable switching in the constrained physical family is not supplied by unrestricted ring ordering.')],
'Stewart’s Theorem 5.5 permits general coupling coefficients; it is not a replacement for positivity and equilibrium constraints. Much of the finite-grid proof can be reduced to the completed square plus one adjacent-distance identity, with no external theorem needed for elementary algebra.',
'The quadratic-cosine selection method has a close precedent in van Kekem and Sterk (2018, Proposition 5). Here T=G−H. If T≥0, R(1)−R(u)=(1−u)[C+2T(1+u)]>0 for u<1. If T<0, R(u)=R(u*)−2(H−G)(u−u*)², with u*=C/[4(H−G)]. Thus the selected representatives are precisely the nearest cosine-grid points, with ties at adjacent midpoints. The physical realizability of these cells is established by the following DM parameter construction.')
add('P06','Large-n limit','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: cite the precedent, retain the sampling estimate.',['VKS'],[
('slot:P06-1; slot:P06-2','Method citation + short estimate','Proposition 6 of van Kekem–Sterk proves a Lorenz–96 limit with vertex 1/4. It does not give the DM limit for a variable vertex or a family depending on n.')],
'Freeze the symbol/parameter family first. For a unique interior continuous maximizer bounded away from the endpoints, grid spacing and nonzero curvature give the familiar angle and objective estimates. Uniform constants and a threshold error need additional uniform curvature, branch regularity and transverse-crossing bounds. Do not import an O(n⁻²) threshold estimate merely from a wavelength limit.',
'Following the finite-grid asymptotic approach of van Kekem and Sterk (2018, Proposition 6), we compare the sampled DM symbol with its continuous maximizer. The remaining proof consists of the grid approximation estimate and the stated uniform derivative bounds for our fixed parameter family. [Insert the actual DM estimates before asserting a rate.]')
add('P07','Five-node specialization','Completed body','Make this a worked corollary of P04–P05.',['Gray'],[
('lem:P07-trig','Standard constants, omit proof','List the fifth-root sine/cosine values. No research-paper citation is needed to justify elementary trigonometry.'),
('thm:P07-spectrum; thm:P07-ordering; cor:P07-selection','Internal specialization','Substitute n=5 in P04/P05; retain the spectrum/order table, remove repeated Fourier and selection proofs.'),
('prop:P07-frequency; prop:P07-intersection; prop:P07-coincidences','Short explicit algebra','Keep the resulting equalities and domain checks; cite P04 for semisimplicity, not for the particular coincidence loci.'),
('cor:P07-stability; prop:P07-ledger','Internal corollary + retain identity','State the maximum test by P04 and keep the model-specific strict negativity at the dominant tie.')],
'An earlier golden-ratio five-agent paper in the audit is historical context, not a theorem proving your DM ordering. Preserve r₀=r₁=−kx*−Cx*/(1+x*)−C/φ²<0 on the physical dominant tie; that short identity does real work.',
'The five-node spectrum and selection table follow by substituting the fifth roots of unity into P04 and specializing P05. On the dominant tie, the equilibrium identity yields r₀=r₁=−kx*−Cx*/(1+x*)−C/φ²<0. Hence this tie lies strictly inside the stable region.')
add('P08','Matched bilinear model and DM–MB comparison','Completed body; includes later global-stability addition','Cite the generic LV method; retain the matched-law comparison theorems.',['Goh','Gray','WVS','Lin'],[
('thm:P08-foundation; prop:P08-bounds','Short standard calculation','Keep multiplicative positivity, M=k+g+h−c and the scalar logistic solution. Use the one-line maximum inequality for full-system bounds; scalar blow-up examples are not a full classification of nonsynchronous trajectories.'),
('thm:P08-spectrum; cor:P08-stability','Direct citation + calculation','Use Gray for the circulant interaction matrix. Keep the exact coefficient threshold Kₙ,q and the population inequality if dissipativity is stated.'),
('thm:P08-global-interior','Method citation + short independent proof','Attribute the LV entropy/Lyapunov argument to Goh. Replace the long compactness/LaSalle exposition by the short proof below. Full Goh theorem text is not verified here, so retain the cancellation and properness sentence.'),
('thm:P08-selection','Reuse P05 algebra','Apply the already established nearest-cosine argument with T=−(g+h), u*=c/[4(g+h)]. Retain MB admissibility and exact cell stability conditions.'),
('prop:P08-structural; thm:P08-pair-realization; thm:P08-forward; thm:P08-reverse; prop:P08-lower','Retain model-specific proofs','No reviewed general theorem supplies the same-tuple constructions, opposite-stability witnesses or branch/parity obstructions.'),
('prop:P08-open; cor:P08-global-contrast','Standard consequence / internal corollary','Use strict inequalities and branch regularity for openness; apply the shortened global MB theorem to the already proved reverse regimes.')],
'The key hypothesis is δ=k−Kₙ,q>0 including mode zero. Establish y*=a/(k+g+h−c)>0 and negative definiteness of (B+Bᵀ)/2. Global attraction is relative to the strictly positive interior; invariant coordinate faces prevent convergence to a positive equilibrium. Lin–Han establishes the five-node model ancestry only: the source coefficient assignment is (a_L,b_L,c_L,d_L,g_L,h_L)=(a,k,c,h,0,g).',
'Write B=−kI+cP−gP^(−q)−hP^q and ȳ=y*1. The Fourier eigenvalues of B_sym=(B+Bᵀ)/2 are −k+c cosθₘ−(g+h)cos(qθₘ)≤−δ. Following Goh’s Lotka–Volterra Lyapunov method, set V=Σᵢ[yᵢ−y*−y*log(yᵢ/y*)]. Then V̇=(y−ȳ)ᵀB_sym(y−ȳ)≤−δ‖y−ȳ‖². Its sublevels are compact within the positive orthant, so continuation and LaSalle’s principle give global interior convergence and stability. A second positive equilibrium would contradict strict decay. Coordinate faces are invariant, which explains the restriction on initial states.')
add('P09','Transcritical bifurcation and the cubic-degenerate case','Completed body','Invoke local theory; delete duplicate verification rather than the model coefficients.',['GH','GLO'],[
('lem:P09-gap','Internal citation','Refer to P03/P04 and display δₙ; omit the repeated Fourier proof.'),
('thm:P09-reduction','IFT + short uniqueness argument','Keep Π⊥F(r1+y,μ), invertibility on E, and T(r,0,μ)=0. IFT uniqueness gives y=0; do not reprove the theorem.'),
('prop:P09-coefficients; thm:P09-transcritical; cor:P09-physical','Standard theorem + calculated coefficients','Retain fᵣμ=1, fᵣᵣ=2b and the stable transverse gap; cite local bifurcation theory. State r*′(0)=−1/b and the g orientation. The exact coordinate/time transformation is optional unless exact orbital equivalence itself is a stated result.'),
('prop:P09-generic-critical; prop:P09-degenerate','Center-manifold reduction; remove duplicate proof','Keep the scalar sign/cubic computation. For b=μ=0, invoke the center-manifold stability reduction principle. Delete the independent full-system Lyapunov derivation if only stability is required.'),
('prop:P09-codimension','Short rank check','Keep the two independent gradients and invoke the regular-level-set theorem; no long proof needed.')],
'For the cubic-degenerate case retain −Ar³/(1+r), the square-root branches and physical-domain restriction. If nonexponential convergence remains in the statement, keep the scalar r⁻² derivative/asymptotic argument. The normal transcritical theorem does not cover b=0.',
'At μ=0 the uniform kernel is simple and the complementary spectrum lies in Re z≤−δₙ. Synchrony is an exact center manifold. Its restriction f(r,μ)=r(μ+br−Ar²)/(1+r) satisfies fᵣμ(0,0)=1 and fᵣᵣ(0,0)=2b, so the standard transcritical criterion applies for b≠0 (Guckenheimer–Holmes, local bifurcation theory). At b=μ=0 the center equation is −Ar³/(1+r); its asymptotically stable origin and the strictly stable complement imply full local asymptotic stability by the center-manifold reduction principle.')
add('P10','Positive fold, bistability and the local basin boundary','Completed body','Keep the transverse identity; cite the fold and invariant-manifold machinery.',['Fold','GH','Guil'],[
('lem:P10-gap','Retain model-specific proof','Keep G_f−H_f=r_f(k+c/s_f²)>0 and the resulting all-q transverse gap. The cited fold theorem assumes the spectral condition; it does not prove it.'),
('prop:P10-reduction','Reuse P09 reduction argument','Reference a shared transverse-IFT lemma; supply J_f|E invertible. No second full copy is needed.'),
('prop:P10-coefficients; thm:P10-saddle-node','Direct standard criterion','Retain α_SN=−r_f/s_f and β_SN=−Ar_f/s_f, then invoke Kuznetsov’s saddle-node criterion. If exact center time-equivalence is retained, its short factor check must stay.'),
('prop:P10-branch-signs; cor:P10-near-stability; thm:P10-bistability','Short calculation + continuity','Keep the branch eigenvalue signs, g_f>g_c and the origin’s stability; persistence of the strict transverse gap is standard.'),
('prop:P10-bistability-set; explicit margin/counterexample','Retain model-specific estimates','Your sufficient parameter interval and the n=6,q=1 unstable-upper-root counterexample are not consequences of a generic fold theorem.'),
('prop:P10-ray','Scalar phase line','Retain the scalar factorization and signs; collapse the proof to a phase-line argument.'),
('prop:P10-local-basins','Manifold theorem + retained connection argument','Cite stable/unstable manifold theory for the disk and tangencies. Keep the identification of its two sides with the two particular sinks; codimension alone does not establish this.')],
'Guilberteau’s global cyclic-feedback results have different hypotheses. They do not justify global DM bistability or a global basin partition. The local exit-cap argument can be shortened, but its role in connecting both basin sides cannot be replaced by merely naming the stable-manifold theorem.',
'The identity G_f−H_f=r_f(k+c/s_f²)>0 makes every nonuniform real part strictly negative, while λ₀=0 is simple. With v=1 and wᵀ=1ᵀ/n, direct differentiation gives wᵀF_g=−r_f/s_f≠0 and ½wᵀF_xx[v,v]=−Ar_f/s_f≠0. The standard multidimensional saddle-node criterion (Kuznetsov, 2006) therefore applies. Continuity preserves transverse stability on the nearby branches; their uniform eigenvalue signs determine the local sink–saddle pair.')
add('P11','Spectral Hopf candidates and admissible cells','Completed body; spectral only','Reduce the generic spectral consequences; keep the physical iff criterion.',['Gray','Pais','Hopf'],[
('lem:P11-pairs; prop:P11-frequency','Internal corollary / trigonometric identity','Refer to the P05 reachable range and factor the P04 imaginary part. No separate general theorem is necessary.'),
('thm:P11-strict; prop:P11-vectors','Short consequence of P04/P05','Strict dominance, rⱼ=0 and ωⱼ≠0 isolate the pair. Normality gives the adjoint vector; the stable complement excludes zero and 2iωⱼ from the spectrum.'),
('prop:P11-surface','Standard level-set theorem + retain derivative','Keep ∂Rⱼ/∂k=−x and the physical positivity checks. This chart derivative is not the total derivative along the frozen g path.'),
('prop:P11-realizability','Retain complete model-specific proof','Keep both directions, wⱼ>0 and max(0,βⱼ)<Bⱼ, the prescribed-vertex inequality and the excluded (8,1)/(12,2) cells.'),
('thm:P11-five; prop:P11-data; prop:P11-ledger','Explicit witness + internal consequences','Keep exact equilibrium/spectrum/parameter checks. Reduce vectors/resolvents to P04 and use IFT for the branch. Preserve the limits on what has been established.')],
'Pais et al. Theorem 4.5 and Remark 1 are valuable attribution for feasible versus algebraic critical parameters, not a theorem proving your cell elimination. P11 is not yet a Hopf bifurcation theorem: the supplied version explicitly leaves transversality, l₁ and periodic orbits to P12–P14.',
'By P04 and strict selection in P05, the only eigenvalues on the imaginary axis are the distinct values ±iωⱼ; all others have strictly negative real part. The critical values are simple, and J and 2iωⱼI−J are invertible. These verify the spectral inputs of the Hopf theorem. Physical admissibility is settled separately by the following DM cellwise criterion; the remaining crossing and nonlinear conditions are addressed in P12–P13.')
add('P12','Hopf transversality','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: cite eigenvalue sensitivity; compute the total derivative.',['GLO','Hopf'],[
('slot:P12-1; slot:P12-2; slot:P12-3','Direct theorem application + model calculation','Use Greenbaum–Li–Overton, Theorem 1, for the derivative of a simple eigenvalue of an analytic matrix family. Analyticity holds for rational DM along an analytic physical path and a regular analytic equilibrium branch; an arbitrary merely smooth path needs the corresponding smooth version.')],
'Compute x*′ and J′ including equilibrium motion; verify a nonzero real derivative on the actual chosen g-path. A genericity statement or partial derivative at fixed x* is insufficient.',
'Implicit differentiation gives Jx*′+F_g=0. For the total matrix family A(g)=D_xF(x*(g),g), A′=F_xg+F_xx[x*′,·]. With p*v=1 and a simple critical eigenvalue, Greenbaum, Li and Overton (2020, Theorem 1) gives λ′=p*A′v. [Insert the exact value or proved nonzero sign of Re λ′ on the designated DM path.]')
add('P13','First Lyapunov coefficient','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: import the formula and method, not another model’s coefficient or sign.',['Hopf','Kerin','Pais'],[
('slot:P13-1','Retain derivative evaluation','Calculate the DM quadratic and cubic tensors using the stated Taylor factorials.'),
('slot:P13-2','Cite method + verify mode factors','Kerin–Engler Appendix A, Proposition 4 supplies a quadratic Fourier interaction rule; Pais et al. Appendix D supplies a cubic-containing precedent. Keep the DM multilinear coefficients, modular aliasing and normalization.'),
('slot:P13-3; slot:P13-4','Direct normal-form formula + model sign proof','Invoke Kuznetsov’s l₁ formula; do not rederive the general normal-form machinery. Retain all DM contractions, nonvanishing/sign bounds and Hopf-hypothesis checks.')],
'DM is rational, so a purely quadratic formula with the third derivative discarded is generally wrong. Choose the eigenvector for +iω₀ with ω₀>0; if the selected label has negative signed frequency, use its conjugate. The cubic and resolvent contractions are the model-specific content.',
'We use the standard first-Lyapunov-coefficient formula of Kuznetsov (2006), with F=Az+½B(z,z)+⅙C(z,z,z)+… and p*v=1. The Fourier reduction follows the approach of Kerin–Engler (§3.2 and Appendix A) and Pais et al. (Appendix D). For DM the cubic tensor is retained. The remaining calculation evaluates these tensors and establishes the stated sign of l₁.')
add('P14','Rotating waves and phase clusters','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: apply equivariant Hopf after the DM hypotheses are proved.',['Stewart','FG'],[
('slot:P14-1','Direct framework application','Use Stewart, Theorem 3.7, for equivariant Hopf; Filipski–Golubitsky is a research-paper reference for the abelian symmetry framework.'),
('slot:P14-2; slot:P14-3','Short representation calculation','Compute the character of the chosen cyclic generator, its kernel, cluster count and signed temporal shift. A cosine ansatz proves only a leading-order expression.')],
'Keep F(Px)=PF(x), the verified simple pair/crossing/nonlinear nondegeneracy, and the precise action convention. The phase classes number n/gcd(n,m); each contains gcd(n,m) nodes for the small nondegenerate mode-m branch. Reconcile the time-shift sign rather than copying a displayed convention from another paper.',
'The homogeneous DM field is Cₙ-equivariant. Once P11–P13 verify nondegenerate Hopf in mode m, equivariant Hopf theory (Stewart, Theorem 3.7; see also Filipski–Golubitsky) gives the corresponding periodic branch with cyclic spatiotemporal symmetry. Its phase classes and temporal shift follow from the character of P on v^(m), using the project’s fixed orientation.')
add('P15','Persistence under heterogeneity','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: IFT can replace almost the entire existence argument.',['GLO','GH'],[
('slot:P15-1','Direct standard theorem','For an explicitly specified analytic heterogeneous family, invoke the analytic IFT stated in Greenbaum–Li–Overton §3.2; otherwise use the smooth IFT.'),
('slot:P15-2','Short continuity argument','A positive base equilibrium stays positive nearby. Stability persistence needs a Hurwitz base Jacobian, not mere invertibility.'),
('slot:P15-3','Separate conditional theorem','Periodic-orbit persistence requires hyperbolicity modulo phase; an equilibrium IFT does not establish it.')],
'Define where every perturbed coefficient enters the model. Retain invertibility checks, the local neighborhood, admissible positivity and any quantitative radius. Uniqueness is local. A Hopf equilibrium may have invertible J even though it is not hyperbolic.',
'At the homogeneous base, det J≠0. The implicit-function theorem therefore yields a unique nearby equilibrium x*(ε) with the regularity of the specified family. Positivity follows by continuity from x*(0)>0. If J is Hurwitz, sufficiently small perturbations preserve local exponential stability by continuity of the spectrum.')
add('P16','Static sensitivity and Green function','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: cite inverse diagonalization; retain the physical forcing and any localization estimate.',['Gray','GLO','BR'],[
('slot:P16-1','IFT differentiation','Keep the forcing b=F_ε determined by the chosen perturbation assignment and use x*′=−J⁻¹b.'),
('slot:P16-2','Direct linear-algebra corollary','Use the P04 framework for inverse Fourier multipliers and ‖J⁻¹‖₂=1/min|λₘ|. No new diagonalization proof.'),
('slot:P16-3','Conditional theorem application','Benzi–Razouk, Theorem 3.4, can supply generic graph-distance decay if its analytic-domain and spectral-set hypotheses are verified for f(z)=1/z.'),
('slot:P16-4','Retain bounds','Taylor’s theorem supplies a remainder form; explicit constants, uniformity in n and directional improvement require your own estimates.')],
'Pointwise invertibility does not by itself verify a zero-avoiding analytic region with uniform constants. Respect wraparound and directed graph distance. A nonzero-frequency Hopf crossing need not make the static inverse singular.',
'Differentiating the equilibrium equation yields ξ=−J⁻¹b. In the P04 Fourier basis this reads ξ̂ₘ=−b̂ₘ/λₘ, with ‖ξ‖₂≤‖b‖₂/minₘ|λₘ|. This is an application of circulant inversion (Gray, Theorem 3.1). Any claimed spatial decay is justified separately by verifying the hypotheses of Benzi–Razouk, Theorem 3.4, or by the sharper model-specific kernel estimate stated below.')
add('P17','Transient and harmonic response','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: present as corollaries of the single Fourier framework lemma.',['Gray'],[
('slot:P17-1','Internal functional-calculus corollary','Write e^(tJ) in the P04 basis; reuse the exact norm identity.'),
('slot:P17-2','Short spectral-expansion argument','Require nonzero projection onto the leading eigenspace and account for ties. No full new theorem is needed.'),
('slot:P17-3','Short substitution + resolvent','For explicitly added harmonic forcing, substitute z=e^(iΩt)ẑ; then ẑ=(iΩI−J)⁻¹b if that inverse exists.'),
('slot:P17-4','Retain scope and remainder work','Asymptotic steady forced response requires stability; nonlinear error control is not supplied by the linear resolvent.')],
'Normal-matrix norm decay is not coordinatewise monotonicity. Resonant response depends on forcing projection. Avoid claiming nonlinear periodic states or global dynamics solely from a linear response formula.',
'Applying the scalar exponential and resolvent to the P04 diagonalization gives exp(tJ)=Q diag(exp(tλₘ))Q* and (iΩI−J)⁻¹=Q diag((iΩ−λₘ)⁻¹)Q*. The claimed linear responses follow by substitution. For a Hurwitz base the homogeneous transient decays, leaving the unique steady harmonic response.')
add('P18','Stability radius and Hopf-threshold shift','TODO in supplied package','Prospective: cite perturbation theory; retain the map from physical parameters to the moving Jacobian.',['ET','GLO','Qiu'],[
('slot:P18-1','Retain model-specific bound','Control J(ε)−J₀ including both coefficient variation and equilibrium displacement.'),
('slot:P18-2','Direct perturbation inclusion','For normal Hurwitz J₀ with margin γ=−max Re λₘ, the inclusion in Embree–Trefethen gives stability when ‖J(ε)−J₀‖₂<γ. This is sufficient, not generally a sharp parameter radius.'),
('slot:P18-3','Eigenvalue derivative + IFT','Use Greenbaum–Li–Overton, Theorem 1, then differentiate Re λ(g_H(ε),ε)=0 with a nonzero g derivative.'),
('slot:P18-4','Retain hypothesis persistence','Check smooth/analytic branch regularity, the remaining spectrum, crossing and l₁ nondegeneracy; a spectral shift alone is not a nonlinear Hopf theorem.')],
'Qiu et al. is appropriate only after matching its full-block uncertainty model. Repeated or diagonal physical parameters and nonlinear equilibrium motion cannot be identified with that model without proof. The stability-radius calculation starts at a strictly stable base, whereas the Hopf shift starts at a critical one.',
'Let γ=−max Re σ(J₀)>0. The normal-matrix perturbation bound of Embree–Trefethen implies that every perturbation E with ‖E‖₂<γ preserves Hurwitz stability. We therefore only need the stated DM estimate on E=J(ε)−J₀. Separately, at a simple transverse Hopf point, Greenbaum–Li–Overton’s derivative formula and the implicit-function theorem give g_H′(0)=−Re[p*J_εv]/Re[p*J_gv], where both derivatives include equilibrium motion.')

css='''body{margin:0;background:#f4f6f8;color:#172334;font:16px/1.65 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif}main{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:48px 32px}h1{font-size:40px;line-height:1.15;letter-spacing:-1px}h2{font-size:25px;line-height:1.3;margin-top:0}h3{font-size:18px}a{color:#135aa0}section{background:white;border:1px solid #dce3e9;border-radius:12px;padding:28px;margin:24px 0}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px}th,td{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #dce3e9;padding:12px 10px}th{background:#eef3f7}td:first-child{width:24%}code{font-size:12px;overflow-wrap:anywhere;color:#38506a}.note{background:#eaf2fa;border-left:4px solid #2874ac;padding:16px 20px}.sample{background:#f5f5f0;padding:18px 22px;border-left:3px solid #8b9454}.status{font-size:13px;color:#54647a}.refs p{margin:8px 0}.small{font-size:13px;color:#506074}nav{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}nav a{background:white;padding:6px 12px;border:1px solid #ccd6e0;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none}button{padding:9px 16px;border:0;border-radius:6px;background:#163d64;color:white;cursor:pointer}@media print{body{background:white;font-size:10pt}main{padding:0;max-width:none}section{border:0;padding:0;margin:20px 0;break-before:page}h1{font-size:28pt}nav,button{display:none}table{font-size:9pt}tr,.sample{break-inside:avoid}a{color:inherit}.refs{break-before:page}}'''
parts=['<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Proof shortening and citation guide — cyclic networks</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body><main>',
'<p class="status">Cyclic network project · Proof-to-citation guide · 4 October 2026</p><h1>What to cite, what to shorten,<br>and what to keep proving</h1>',
'<p>The main opportunity is to replace general mathematical machinery with citations, then retain the short calculation that verifies its hypotheses for DM or MB. A result can be routine without being an exact theorem in another paper. In that case, compress the algebra rather than attribute the DM statement to the wrong model.</p>',
'<p><button onclick="window.print()">Print / save as PDF</button></p>',
'<div class="note"><strong>Scope and version.</strong> This guide uses the supplied HTML prior-art audit, the archive’s earlier literature reviews, and the actual proof source files. The HTML audit itself was based on a blueprint; its judgments are checked here against those source bodies. P01–P05 and P07–P11 contain completed proofs. P06 and P12–P18 are TODO outlines in this package. Their sections below are prospective; no completed proof is claimed. P08 includes a later global-interior-stability addition. Older planning statements saying every module is TODO are stale. This is an editorial citation assessment, not a new independent mathematical audit or a certificate of novelty. The supplied project files were not modified.</div>',
'<p><strong>How to read the recommendations.</strong> “Direct theorem application” means the general result can replace its reproof after its hypotheses are checked. “Method citation” credits a related method while retaining the DM/MB adaptation. “Internal corollary” means cite your own earlier lemma. “Retain” means the reviewed sources do not supply that model-specific claim. Exact labels below are source-file labels, so they remain usable if theorem numbers change.</p>',
'<nav>'+''.join(f'<a href="#{m["id"]}">{m["id"]}</a>' for m in modules)+'<a href="#references">References & evidence</a></nav>',
'<section><h2>Highest-value edits</h2><table><tr><th>Where</th><th>Change</th><th>What remains</th></tr>',
'<tr><td>P03–P04, P07, P08, P11</td><td>Establish one Fourier framework lemma using Gray, Theorem 3.1; reuse it throughout.</td><td>Actual Jacobian coefficients, shift signs, model-specific equalities and inequalities.</td></tr>',
'<tr><td>P08 global interior stability</td><td>Credit Goh and retain a compact entropy calculation instead of the full general Lyapunov/LaSalle exposition.</td><td>Positive equilibrium, negative symmetric part, compact sublevels and interior scope.</td></tr>',
'<tr><td>P09/P10 local reductions</td><td>Use one transverse-IFT argument, cite center/stable-manifold theory and the relevant bifurcation criterion.</td><td>Simple kernel, strict transverse gap, nonzero coefficients and physical branch orientation.</td></tr>',
'<tr><td>P09 cubic-degenerate stability</td><td>Remove the second, independent full-system Lyapunov proof after invoking center-manifold stability reduction.</td><td>The exact center equation and nonexponential rate argument if that rate is claimed.</td></tr>',
'<tr><td>P05/P07 algebra and tables</td><td>Keep formulas/tables, compress repeated derivations and use internal references.</td><td>Parity, realizability, physical switch geometry and strict negativity on the five-node tie.</td></tr>',
'<tr><td>P12–P18, if developed</td><td>Start from cited formulas and theorems rather than deriving general machinery.</td><td>Actual parameter derivatives, tensors, sign checks, remainder bounds and structural hypotheses.</td></tr></table>',
'<p><strong>Preserve the strongest model-specific proofs:</strong> P05’s positive realization and switch geometry; P08’s shared-parameter, branch and parity comparison constructions; P10’s automatic transverse fold stability and explicit coexistence estimates; P11’s cellwise physical-realizability iff criterion. Similar methods elsewhere do not remove these obligations.</p></section>']
for m in modules:
 p=next(Path('work/source/cyclic_network_project/proofs').glob(m['id']+'_*'))
 parts += [f'<section id="{m["id"]}"><p class="status">{m["status"]} · {html.escape(p.name)}</p><h2>{m["id"]} — {m["title"]}</h2><p><strong>{m["verdict"]}</strong></p>', '<p>Sources: '+ '; '.join(cite(k) for k in m['sources'])+'. Exact locators and access limits are in the reference list.</p>', '<table><tr><th>Proof / source label</th><th>Treatment</th><th>Concrete edit</th></tr>']
 for label,action,desc in m['rows']: parts.append('<tr><td>'+code(label)+'</td><td>'+html.escape(action)+'</td><td>'+html.escape(desc)+'</td></tr>')
 parts += ['</table><h3>What still needs to remain</h3><p>'+html.escape(m['keep'])+'</p><h3>Suggested shortened wording</h3><div class="sample">'+html.escape(m['snippet'])+'</div></section>']
parts += ['<section><h2>A compact manuscript structure</h2><p>Combine P01–P04 into a foundation section: one positivity/bound proposition, one synchronous-root proposition, and one Jacobian/Fourier lemma. Develop P05, P08 and P11 with their substantive constructions intact. Present P07 as a worked five-node example. Combine the general reduction tools used in P09/P10 into a short preliminary lemma; keep each model-specific nondegeneracy calculation next to its application.</p><p>Put extended weak-sign P01 cases, negative-root bookkeeping, long numerical-coincidence tables and redundant verification calculations in a supplement if they are useful for reproducibility. This is a proposed presentation change, not deletion of established claims. Do not merge all modules into a claim that Hopf or global DM dynamics are already proved.</p><p>In particular, avoid “the proof is the same as [paper]” when the paper has a different coupling law. Prefer “we apply [general theorem]; its hypotheses follow from [displayed DM identities]” or “following the method of [paper], the remaining calculation is …”.</p></section>', '<section id="references" class="refs"><h2>References, precise locations and evidence limits</h2><p>The sources below come from your supplied review materials. Primary web checks were used to confirm the principal citation candidates. A source listed only as contextual or not fully accessible is not treated as a verified theorem-level substitute. Audit B-numbers refer to the HTML report; S-numbers refer to the earlier TeX literature reviews.</p>']
for k,(a,t,u,loc,e) in refs.items():parts.append(f'<div id="ref-{k}"><h3>{html.escape(a)}</h3><p><a href="{html.escape(u)}">{html.escape(t)}</a></p><p><strong>Locator:</strong> {html.escape(loc)}</p><p class="small">{html.escape(e)}</p></div>')
parts += ['<h3>Sources not suitable for automatic proof replacement</h3><p>The inaccessible or only partly inspected Lin–Shi–Han 2021/2022, Lin–Han 2023/2024 and related thesis materials remain priority leads, not theorem-level proof substitutes. The monotone cyclic-feedback and repressilator papers do not automatically cover the multi-input DM ring. Golden-ratio literature supplies context, not the nonlinear DM stable-tie identity. Demko-type inverse-decay results should not be imported merely because the matrix looks banded; the Benzi–Razouk graph-distance framework is the better reviewed starting point, with its hypotheses checked.</p>', '<h3>Provenance</h3><p class="small">Inputs: prior-art-audit.html and audit-data.json; cyclic_network_project/03_literature_audit.tex and 04_adversarial_novelty_audit.tex; references.bib; canonical model; actual P01–P18 source fragments. Project-internal directions and historical workflow gates were treated as document content, not instructions for this task. Suggested wording is newly written and is not a quotation from the cited papers.</p></section></main></body></html>']
(out/'proof-shortening-guide.html').write_text('\n'.join(parts))
# Searchable, spreadsheet-compatible proof-level ledger without modifying manuscript sources.
import csv
with (out/'proof-citation-map.csv').open('w',newline='') as f:
 w=csv.writer(f);w.writerow(['Module','Status','Proof labels','Treatment','Concrete edit','Citations','Keep / limitations'])
 for m in modules:
  for label,action,desc in m['rows']:w.writerow([m['id'],m['status'],label,action,desc,'; '.join(refs[k][0]+' — '+refs[k][2] for k in m['sources']),m['keep']])
# Validation: module coverage, unique anchors, source-label accuracy.
s='\n'.join(parts); ids=re.findall(r'id="([^"]+)"',s)
assert len(ids)==len(set(ids))
assert all(x in ids for x in re.findall(r'href="#([^"]+)"',s))
assert len(modules)==18
errors=[]
for m in modules:
 src=next(Path('work/source/cyclic_network_project/proofs').glob(m['id']+'_*')).read_text()
 for label,_,_ in m['rows']:
  for lab in re.findall(r'(?:lem|prop|thm|cor|eq|slot):P\d\d-[\w-]+',label):
   if '\\label{'+lab+'}' not in src:errors.append(lab)
assert not errors,errors
print(json.dumps({'modules':len(modules),'proof_groups':sum(len(m['rows']) for m in modules),'references':len(refs),'output_bytes':len(s.encode()),'source_labels_verified':True},indent=2))
